window.GIQ={"meta":{"tier":1,"lede":"","top":29,"fac":{"n":29,"nsrc":2}},"lectures":[{"id":"HIST-11","t":"Digestive tract (H15A, H15B)"}],"groups":[{"l":"HIST-11","u":0,"label":"Filiform papillae: pointed, keratinized, no taste buds","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G018-17.jpg","cap":"Four papilla types: filiform panel pointed and keratinized, without taste buds","where":"2026 digestive lab deck, slide 17","lg":[["(c) arrows","deep clefts between the foliate ridges"],["(b) arrows","secondary connective tissue papillae pushing up into the epithelium"]]},{"src":"../sl/G015-35.jpg","cap":"Tongue surface: pointed filiform (FI) beside mushroom-shaped fungiform (F) papillae","where":"2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 35","lg":[["a","dorsal tongue surface with filiform and fungiform papillae"],["F","fungiform papilla (mushroom-shaped, lightly keratinized)"],["FI","filiform papillae (pointed, heavily keratinized)"],["SS","stratified squamous epithelium"],["CT","connective tissue core"],["b","one large vallate papilla"],["TB","taste buds on the papilla's side walls"],["GL","small serous (von Ebner) glands draining into the trench"]]}],"q":[{"id":"G018-49-1","s":"Q2. Name the type of papillae:","o":["A. Filiform papillae","B. Foliate papillae","C. Fungiform papillae","D. Circumvallate papillae"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Filiform papillae","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Filiform papillae.</b> Rows of slender, pointed cones capped with keratin (the pale layer at the tips). They have no taste buds. They give the tongue its rough surface and are the most numerous papillae.<br>B. Foliate: parallel ridges separated by deep clefts, with taste buds on the walls.<br>C. Fungiform: mushroom-shaped, with a connective-tissue core and taste buds on top.<br>D. Circumvallate: one large papilla in a circular trench, with taste buds on its walls.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/G018-49.jpg","in":"Row of pointed, slanted papillae capped with keratin; no taste buds. Label blacked out.","tg":["H15A slide 39 (The tongue)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 lab","n":"Digestive system lab deck Fall 2026 (practice questions)","sl":49,"f":"G018"}],"tier":1,"n":"1"}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":0,"label":"Circumvallate papilla: trench, taste buds, glands","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G015-35.jpg","cap":"Vallate papilla: trench, taste buds (TB) on walls, glands (GL) below","where":"2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 35","lg":[["a","dorsal tongue surface with filiform and fungiform papillae"],["F","fungiform papilla (mushroom-shaped, lightly keratinized)"],["FI","filiform papillae (pointed, heavily keratinized)"],["SS","stratified squamous epithelium"],["CT","connective tissue core"],["b","one large vallate papilla"],["TB","taste buds on the papilla's side walls"],["GL","small serous (von Ebner) glands draining into the trench"]]},{"src":"../sl/G018-17.jpg","cap":"Circumvallate papilla with taste buds on its walls and serous glands beneath","where":"2026 digestive lab deck, slide 17","lg":[["(c) arrows","deep clefts between the foliate ridges"],["(b) arrows","secondary connective tissue papillae pushing up into the epithelium"]]}],"q":[{"id":"G018-48-1","s":"Q1. Name the type of papillae:","o":["A. Filiform papillae","B. Foliate papillae","C. Fungiform papillae","D. Circumvallate papillae"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Circumvallate papillae","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Circumvallate papillae.</b> One large, flat-topped papilla sunk below the surface in a circular trench. Taste buds line its side walls, and serous (von Ebner) glands below it empty into the trench. These papillae form the V-shaped row just in front of the sulcus terminalis.<br>A. Filiform: many small, pointed, keratinized cones with no taste buds.<br>B. Foliate: several parallel ridges with clefts between them, on the sides of the tongue.<br>C. Fungiform: a single mushroom-shaped papilla raised above the surface, with taste buds on its top.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/G018-48.jpg","in":"One large flat-topped papilla in a deep trench; taste buds labeled on its walls; serous glands labeled below. The name of the papilla is blacked out.","tg":["H15A slide 39 (The tongue)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 lab","n":"Digestive system lab deck Fall 2026 (practice questions)","sl":48,"f":"G018"}],"tier":1,"n":"2"}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":0,"label":"Foliate papillae: parallel ridges with taste-bud clefts","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G018-17.jpg","cap":"Foliate papillae: parallel ridges, taste buds in clefts, serous glands below","where":"2026 digestive lab deck, slide 17","lg":[["(c) arrows","deep clefts between the foliate ridges"],["(b) arrows","secondary connective tissue papillae pushing up into the epithelium"]]},{"src":"../sl/G015-33.jpg","cap":"Diagrams of filiform, fungiform, vallate and foliate papillae","where":"2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 33"}],"q":[{"id":"G018-50-1","s":"Q3. Name the type of papillae:","o":["A. Filiform papillae","B. Foliate papillae","C. Fungiform papillae","D. Circumvallate papillae"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Foliate papillae","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Foliate papillae.</b> Several parallel, leaf-like ridges separated by deep, narrow clefts. Taste buds line the walls of the clefts, and ducts of serous glands open into the clefts. They sit on the sides of the back of the tongue.<br>A. Filiform: pointed keratinized cones, no taste buds.<br>C. Fungiform: a single mushroom shape, not a row of ridges with clefts.<br>D. Circumvallate: one large papilla surrounded by a circular trench, not several parallel ridges.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/G018-50.jpg","in":"Several parallel ridges separated by narrow clefts (arrows); taste buds, ducts and serous glands labeled. Label blacked out.","tg":["H15A slide 39 (The tongue)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 lab","n":"Digestive system lab deck Fall 2026 (practice questions)","sl":50,"f":"G018"}],"tier":1,"lg":[["arrows","deep clefts between the foliate ridges"],["lines at bottom (label cut off)","striated (skeletal) muscle"]],"n":"3"}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":1,"label":"Taste bud cells and microvilli in the pore","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G015-36.jpg","cap":"Taste bud diagram: gustatory pore, microvilli, support, gustatory and basal cells","where":"2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 36"},{"src":"../sl/G018-18.jpg","cap":"Taste bud: gustatory microvilli reach the taste pore; supporting and basal cells","where":"2026 digestive lab deck, slide 18","lg":[["pink arrows","lead from each boxed area to its enlargement"],["orange arrow","taste buds (pale ovals) in the side wall of the papilla"]]}],"q":[{"id":"F3955-26-1","s":"Name the structures numbered as #1, #2 and #3, respectively:","o":["A. Supporting cells; Cilia; Sensory cells","B. Supporting cells; Taste pore; Sensory cells","C. Goblet cells; Stereocilia; Supporting cells","D. Sensory cells; Microvilli; Basal cells","E. Basal cells; Taste pore; Sensory cells"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Sensory cells; Microvilli; Basal cells","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Sensory cells; microvilli; basal cells.</b> #1 marks the pale elongated <b>gustatory (sensory) cells</b> inside the bud. #2 marks their <b>microvilli</b> reaching into the taste pore. #3 marks a small <b>basal (stem) cell</b> at the base, above the nerve.<br>A. Supporting cells; cilia: #1 are sensory cells, and taste cells bear microvilli, not cilia.<br>B. Taste pore for #2: #2 points to the microvilli inside the pore.<br>C. Goblet cells; stereocilia: neither is in a taste bud.<br>E. Basal cells for #1: #1 is inside the bud, not at its base.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/G018-51.jpg","in":"High-power taste bud: #1 on elongated pale cells inside the bud, #2 at the apical opening where processes project into the pore, #3 on a small cell at the bud base above the nerve.","tg":["H15A slide 36 (Gustatory pore)","H15A slide 37 (The tongue)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 lab","n":"Digestive system lab deck Fall 2026 (practice questions)","sl":51,"f":"G018"},{"t":"2024 Middletown lab clicker","n":"lab clickers week 10 digestive","sl":26,"f":"F3955"},{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"2022 Histo TA exam review 2: GI part only","sl":72,"f":"F0753"},{"t":"2021 Harlem lab clicker","n":"lab clickers Exam 2: GI part only","sl":145,"f":"F0052"}],"tier":1,"lg":[["1","sensory (gustatory) cells: pale, round nuclei"],["2","gustatory microvilli in the taste pore"],["3","basal (stem) cell"]],"n":"4"}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":2,"label":"Esophagus: nonkeratinized stratified squamous lining","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G015-55.jpg","cap":"Esophagus: thick nonkeratinized stratified squamous epithelium","where":"2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 55"},{"src":"../sl/G018-23.jpg","cap":"Esophagus: squamous epithelium, lamina propria, muscularis mucosae, submucosal mucous glands","where":"2026 digestive lab deck, slide 23","lg":[["SS","stratified squamous epithelium, nonkeratinized"],["LP","lamina propria"],["MM","muscularis mucosae (smooth muscle)"],["D","ducts of the esophageal glands"],["GL","esophageal mucous glands in the submucosa"]]},{"src":"../sl/G018-24.jpg","cap":"Esophagus transmural: squamous lining over inner circular and outer longitudinal muscle","where":"2026 digestive lab deck, slide 24"}],"q":[{"id":"G018-21-1","s":"From which organ could this section have been obtained?","o":["A. Outer surface of lip","B. Inner surface of lip","C. Tongue","D. Oral mucosa","E. Esophagus"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Esophagus","a":"E","why":"<b>E. Esophagus.</b> The lining is non-keratinized stratified squamous epithelium. Under it sit a thin muscularis mucosae, a submucosa with a mucous gland, and then thick skeletal muscle in two layers at right angles (the inner layer cut along its length, the outer layer cut across). A muscularis externa of skeletal muscle in two neat layers is the upper third of the esophagus.<br>A. Outer surface of lip: thin skin, keratinized, with hair follicles and sweat glands.<br>B. Inner surface of lip: also non-keratinized, but it has no muscularis mucosae, and its skeletal muscle (orbicularis oris) is one sheet, not two layers at right angles; labial salivary glands sit under it.<br>C. Tongue: skeletal muscle bundles run in all three planes, and the top surface carries papillae.<br>D. Oral mucosa (cheek, floor of the mouth): no muscularis mucosae and no two-layer muscularis externa.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/G018-21.jpg","in":"Two pictures: low power of a wall with stratified squamous epithelium on one side, glands and vessels beneath, then two layers of skeletal muscle at right angles; high power of the non-keratinized stratified squamous epithelium.","tg":["H15A slide 60 (Mucosa:)","H15A slide 53 (Esophagus)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 lab","n":"Digestive system lab deck Fall 2026 (practice questions)","sl":21,"f":"G018"}],"tier":1,"n":"5"}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":3,"label":"Esophagus thirds: skeletal, mixed, smooth","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G015-60.jpg","cap":"Diagram: upper third skeletal, middle mixed, lower third smooth muscle","where":"2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 60"},{"src":"../sl/G015-59.jpg","cap":"Middle third: big skeletal fibers mixed with smooth muscle bundles","where":"2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 59"},{"src":"../sl/G018-22.jpg","cap":"Esophagus cross-section; text lists the muscularis externa by thirds","where":"2026 digestive lab deck, slide 22"}],"q":[{"id":"F3955-36-1","s":"What portion of the esophagus does this section corresponds to?","o":["A. Upper third","B. Middle third","C. Lower third"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Middle third","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Middle third.</b> The muscularis externa here <b>mixes large pink skeletal fibers</b> (nuclei at the edge of each fiber) <b>with bundles of small smooth muscle cells</b> (one central nucleus each). A mix of both types occurs only in the middle third.<br>A. Upper third: skeletal fibers only.<br>C. Lower third: smooth muscle only.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/G018-52.jpg","in":"Muscularis externa with large pink cross-cut skeletal fibers (peripheral nuclei) mixed with smooth muscle bundles (central nuclei).","tg":["H15A slide 60 (Mucosa:)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 lab","n":"Digestive system lab deck Fall 2026 (practice questions)","sl":52,"f":"G018"},{"t":"2024 Middletown lab clicker","n":"lab clickers week 10 digestive","sl":36,"f":"F3955"},{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"2022 Histo TA exam review 2: GI part only","sl":76,"f":"F0753"},{"t":"2021 Harlem lab clicker","n":"lab clickers Exam 2: GI part only","sl":151,"f":"F0052"},{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"2021 Histology Exam 2 TA review: GI part only","sl":221,"f":"F0755"}],"tier":1,"n":"6"}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":4,"label":"Reflux esophagitis: symptoms, treatment, biopsy","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G015-57.jpg","cap":"Normal esophageal mucosa: squamous epithelium, lamina propria, muscularis mucosae","where":"2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 57","lg":[["SS","nonkeratinized stratified squamous epithelium"],["LP","lamina propria"],["MM","muscularis mucosae (smooth muscle)"],["D","ducts of the esophageal glands"],["GL","esophageal mucous glands in the submucosa"]]},{"src":"../sl/G015-65.jpg","cap":"Reflux disease progression: normal lining, Barrett, dysplasia, invasive carcinoma","where":"2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 65"}],"q":[{"id":"G017-2-1","s":"A 35-year-old overweight man has had months of burning central chest pain after dinner, worse when he reclines, with a sour taste in his mouth. TUMS (an antacid) give some relief. He drinks 4–5 cups of coffee a day and a beer after dinner. What is the most likely diagnosis and best first management?","o":["A. Gastroesophageal reflux disease (reflux esophagitis): smaller evening meals, sitting upright after eating, less coffee and beer, a proton pump inhibitor, and a recheck in 8 weeks","B. Barrett esophagus: immediate esophagectomy","C. Eosinophilic esophagitis: an elimination diet and topical steroids","D. Achalasia: pneumatic dilation of the lower esophageal sphincter","E. Duodenal ulcer: Helicobacter pylori eradication therapy alone"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Gastroesophageal reflux disease (reflux esophagitis): smaller evening meals, sitting upright after eating, less coffee and beer, a proton pump inhibitor, and a recheck in 8 weeks","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Gastroesophageal reflux disease (reflux esophagitis).</b> Burning chest pain after big meals while reclining, a sour taste and relief from antacids mean stomach acid is flowing back through a weak lower esophageal sphincter; coffee, alcohol and extra weight make it worse. First-line care is lifestyle change plus a proton pump inhibitor, with a recheck in 8 weeks.<br>B. Barrett esophagus: a later complication of long-standing reflux, found on biopsy; it is followed with surveillance endoscopy, not removed at once.<br>C. Eosinophilic esophagitis: considered only when reflux treatment fails; it is allergic and often makes food stick.<br>D. Achalasia: trouble swallowing both solids and liquids because the lower sphincter fails to relax, not acid heartburn relieved by antacids.<br>E. Duodenal ulcer: epigastric pain that eases with food, not burning chest pain on reclining after dinner.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15A slide 65 (Esophogeal Metaplasia)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 interactive","n":"Digestive system interactive Fall 2026","sl":2,"f":"G017"},{"t":"2024 clicker","n":"W10 Digestive clicker session","sl":2,"f":"F0702"}],"tier":1,"n":"7"},{"id":"G017-8-1","s":"A 35-year-old man with months of heartburn after dinner has an upper endoscopy. The esophageal biopsy shows stratified squamous epithelium with a thickened basal cell layer, vascular papillae that reach up toward the surface, and scattered eosinophils within the epithelium. What is the diagnosis?","o":["A. Barrett esophagus","B. Reflux esophagitis","C. Esophageal adenocarcinoma","D. Candida esophagitis","E. Achalasia"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Reflux esophagitis","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Reflux esophagitis.</b> Acid injury to the squamous lining gives three classic changes: basal cell hyperplasia (a thicker basal layer from faster turnover), elongated vascular papillae reaching toward the surface, and eosinophils inside the epithelium. The lining is still stratified squamous.<br>A. Barrett esophagus: the squamous lining is replaced by columnar epithelium with goblet cells.<br>C. Adenocarcinoma: invasive malignant glands, usually arising in Barrett mucosa.<br>D. Candida esophagitis: white plaques with yeast and fungal hyphae on the surface.<br>E. Achalasia: a motility disorder from loss of myenteric neurons; the lining is not the problem.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15A slide 65 (Esophogeal Metaplasia)","H15A slide 60 (Mucosa:)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 interactive","n":"Digestive system interactive Fall 2026","sl":8,"f":"G017"},{"t":"2024 clicker","n":"W10 Digestive clicker session","sl":6,"f":"F0702"}],"tier":1,"n":"8"}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":4,"label":"Reflux esophagitis: differential diagnosis","slides":[],"q":[{"id":"G017-4-1","s":"A 35-year-old man treated for 8 weeks with a proton pump inhibitor for presumed reflux still has heartburn and now an occasional cough. Because most reflux responds to this treatment, other causes of esophagitis are considered. Which group lists those other causes?","o":["A. Eosinophilic esophagitis, infection (Candida, herpes simplex virus, cytomegalovirus), pill esophagitis and radiation injury","B. Achalasia, Zenker diverticulum and esophageal webs","C. Barrett esophagus, esophageal varices and Mallory-Weiss tears","D. Hiatal hernia, pyloric stenosis and duodenal ulcer","E. Celiac disease, Crohn disease and ulcerative colitis"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Eosinophilic esophagitis, infection (Candida, herpes simplex virus, cytomegalovirus), pill esophagitis and radiation injury","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Eosinophilic esophagitis, infection, pill esophagitis and radiation injury.</b> Heartburn that does not settle on a proton pump inhibitor may not be acid reflux. The other causes of esophagitis are eosinophilic (allergic) esophagitis, infections (Candida; herpes simplex virus and cytomegalovirus, mostly in immunosuppressed people), pills that injure the lining (NSAIDs, doxycycline, ferrous sulfate) and radiation.<br>B. Achalasia, Zenker diverticulum, webs: these cause trouble swallowing, not inflammation of the lining.<br>C. Barrett esophagus, varices, Mallory-Weiss tears: Barrett is a complication of reflux, varices come from portal hypertension, and Mallory-Weiss tears follow forceful vomiting.<br>D. Hiatal hernia, pyloric stenosis, duodenal ulcer: a hiatal hernia worsens reflux rather than being another cause of esophagitis; the other two are stomach and duodenum problems.<br>E. Celiac disease, Crohn disease, ulcerative colitis: intestinal diseases, not causes of esophagitis.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 interactive","n":"Digestive system interactive Fall 2026","sl":4,"f":"G017"},{"t":"2024 clicker","n":"W10 Digestive clicker session","sl":3,"f":"F0702"}],"tier":1,"n":"9"}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":4,"label":"Metaplasia: one mature epithelium replaces another","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G015-66.jpg","cap":"Metaplasia example: squamous esophagus replaced by columnar (Barrett) epithelium","where":"2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 66"}],"q":[{"id":"G017-12-1","s":"In cigarette smokers, ciliated pseudostratified columnar epithelium lining the airways may be replaced by stratified squamous epithelium. In chronic reflux esophagitis, stratified squamous epithelium lining the esophagus may be replaced by simple columnar epithelium. This replacement of the one epithelial type by another epithelial type in response to injury is referred to as:","o":["A. Neoplasia","B. Metaplasia","C. Dysplasia","D. Differentiation","E. Quiescence"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Metaplasia","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Metaplasia.</b> Metaplasia is the <b>replacement of one mature cell type by another</b> that better withstands an injury: squamous for ciliated cells in a smoker's airway, columnar for squamous in an acid-washed esophagus. It can reverse if the injury stops.<br>A. Neoplasia: uncontrolled new growth (a tumor).<br>C. Dysplasia: disordered, atypical growth; a precancer step after metaplasia.<br>D. Differentiation: normal maturation of a cell.<br>E. Quiescence: a resting, nondividing state.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15A slide 65 (Esophogeal Metaplasia)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 interactive","n":"Digestive system interactive Fall 2026","sl":12,"f":"G017"},{"t":"2024 clicker","n":"W10 Digestive clicker session","sl":8,"f":"F0702"}],"tier":1,"n":"10"}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":5,"label":"Heartburn plus red distal mucosa: Barrett esophagus","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G015-66.jpg","cap":"Normal squamous esophagus versus abnormal columnar Barrett mucosa","where":"2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 66"},{"src":"../sl/G015-65.jpg","cap":"Barrett diagram: goblet cells and glands replacing the squamous lining","where":"2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 65"}],"q":[{"id":"G017-10-1","s":"Three years after his reflux was controlled with a proton pump inhibitor, a man has a surveillance endoscopy. It shows salmon-colored tongues of mucosa extending up from the gastroesophageal junction into the pale squamous lining. The biopsy shows simple columnar epithelium with goblet cells where stratified squamous epithelium should be. What is the diagnosis?","o":["A. Reflux esophagitis","B. Barrett esophagus","C. Eosinophilic esophagitis","D. Hiatal hernia","E. Squamous cell carcinoma of the esophagus"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Barrett esophagus","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Barrett esophagus.</b> After years of reflux, salmon-colored tongues of simple columnar epithelium with goblet cells (intestinal metaplasia) replace the stratified squamous lining of the distal esophagus. It is a response to acid and raises the risk of adenocarcinoma, so it is followed with surveillance endoscopy.<br>A. Reflux esophagitis: the lining stays stratified squamous, with a thick basal layer and eosinophils.<br>C. Eosinophilic esophagitis: a squamous lining crowded with eosinophils.<br>D. Hiatal hernia: part of the stomach slides up through the diaphragm; the biopsy would show ordinary gastric mucosa without goblet cells.<br>E. Squamous cell carcinoma: invasive malignant squamous cells, usually in the middle esophagus, linked to smoking and alcohol.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15A slide 65 (Esophogeal Metaplasia)","H15A slide 66 (Esophogeal Metaplasia)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 interactive","n":"Digestive system interactive Fall 2026","sl":10,"f":"G017"},{"t":"2024 clicker","n":"W10 Digestive clicker session","sl":7,"f":"F0702"}],"tier":1,"n":"11"}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":6,"label":"Stomach: simple columnar surface mucous cells","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G015-70.jpg","cap":"Gastric mucosa: simple columnar surface mucous cells (arrow), no goblet cells","where":"2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 70"},{"src":"../sl/G015-71.jpg","cap":"Gastric pits lined by surface mucous cells","where":"2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 71","lg":[["A","gastric pit lined by surface mucous cells"],["B","gastric gland starting at the base of the pit"],["inset","stomach surface seen by scanning electron microscope"],["P","gastric pit opening (inset)"]]}],"q":[{"id":"G018-53-1","s":"Q6. Which cell type is indicated by the arrow?","o":["A. Enteroendocrine cell","B. Surface mucous cell","C. Enterocyte","D. Mucous neck cell","E. Chief cell"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Surface mucous cell","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Surface mucous cell.</b> The arrow is on the tall columnar cells that line the stomach surface and the gastric pits. Each cell has a pale, mucus-filled top, and the cells form one continuous sheet with no goblet cells between them. They make the thick, alkaline mucus layer that protects the lining from acid.<br>A. Enteroendocrine cell: small, scattered cells deep in the glands, with granules at the base; they need special stains to be seen.<br>C. Enterocyte: the absorptive cell of the intestine, with a brush border, sitting among goblet cells on villi.<br>D. Mucous neck cell: in the neck of the gastric gland, below the pit, not on the surface.<br>E. Chief cell: a purple (basophilic) cell at the base of the fundic glands.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/G018-53.jpg","in":"Gastric surface and pits lined by tall columnar cells with pale apical cytoplasm; arrow on the surface lining.","tg":["H15A slide 76 (Fundic/Body cells)","H15A slide 71 (Gastric Pits)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 lab","n":"Digestive system lab deck Fall 2026 (practice questions)","sl":53,"f":"G018"}],"tier":1,"lg":[["arrow","surface mucous cell (pale mucus cup at the apex, stomach surface)"]],"n":"12"}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":7,"label":"Parietal pink, chief purple on H&E","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G015-78.jpg","cap":"Fundic glands, 400x: pale-pink parietal cells (PC) above purple chief cells (CC)","where":"2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 78","lg":[["box (50x)","area enlarged in the 100x panel"],["box (100x)","gland bases enlarged in the 400x panel"],["PC","parietal cells (pink, round central nucleus)"],["CC","chief cells (purple, at the gland bases)"]]},{"src":"../sl/G018-30.jpg","cap":"Fundus/body mucosa: pits, neck and glands; parietal (P) and chief (C) cells labelled","where":"2026 digestive lab deck, slide 30","lg":[["GP","gastric pits"],["SM","surface mucous cells"],["MN","mucous neck cells"],["P","parietal cells (big, pink, round 'fried egg')"],["C","chief cells (purple, basophilic, deep in the glands)"],["MM","muscularis mucosae"],["boxes","areas enlarged at right"]]}],"q":[{"id":"G018-54-1","s":"Q7. The cell indicated by A produces:","o":["A. Pepsinogen","B. Intrinsic factor","C. Histamine","D. Gastrin","E. Lysozyme"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Intrinsic factor","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Intrinsic factor.</b> Cell A is large and round, stains pale pink (acidophilic, because it is full of mitochondria), and has a round central nucleus: a parietal (oxyntic) cell of a fundic gland. Parietal cells secrete hydrochloric acid (HCl) and intrinsic factor, which is needed to absorb vitamin B12. (Cell B is a purple chief cell.)<br>A. Pepsinogen: made by chief cells (cell B), not by cell A.<br>C. Histamine: made by enterochromaffin-like (ECL) cells, a type of enteroendocrine cell.<br>D. Gastrin: made by G cells in the pyloric (antral) glands.<br>E. Lysozyme: made by Paneth cells of the small intestine (and by pyloric gland cells).","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/G018-54.jpg","in":"Fundic glands: arrow A on a round pink cell with a central nucleus; arrow B on a purple (basophilic) cell lower in the gland.","tg":["H15A slide 76 (Fundic/Body cells)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 lab","n":"Digestive system lab deck Fall 2026 (practice questions)","sl":54,"f":"G018"}],"tier":1,"lg":[["A","parietal cell (pink, round, central nucleus): HCl and intrinsic factor"],["B","chief cell (purple, basophilic, gland base): pepsinogen"]],"n":"13"}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":8,"label":"Enteroendocrine cells: special stains, serotonin","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G018-31.jpg","cap":"Enteroendocrine cells: seen with chromium, silver or immunostains; serotonin increases motility","where":"2026 digestive lab deck, slide 31"},{"src":"../sl/G018-32.jpg","cap":"Electron micrograph: enteroendocrine cell (E) with basal granules beside parietal, chief cells","where":"2026 digestive lab deck, slide 32"}],"q":[{"id":"G017-13-1","s":"A researcher uses an immunostaining preparation to examine a slide from the gastric cardia mucosa. On his immunostained preparation, he can observe scattered cells in the glandular portion not seen in the H&E preparation. Which of the following best applies to these cells?","o":["A. Their secretions may increase motility of the GI tract","B. They ordinarily replace old or damaged gastric epithelial cells","C. They are capable of secreting mucus in the stomach","D. They are required for vitamin B12 absorption","E. Intracellular canaliculi are an ultrastructural feature of these cells"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Their secretions may increase motility of the GI tract","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Their secretions may increase gut motility.</b> Cells seen only with special stains (immunostaining, silver or chromium salts) are <b>enteroendocrine cells</b>. One type, the enterochromaffin cell, secretes <b>serotonin</b>, which increases motility.<br>B. Replacing old cells: stem cells in the isthmus and neck.<br>C. Secreting mucus: surface mucous and mucous neck cells, visible on routine hematoxylin and eosin stain.<br>D. Needed for vitamin B12 absorption: parietal cells (intrinsic factor).<br>E. Intracellular canaliculi: parietal cells.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"Immunostain of gastric glands with scattered dark brown-stained single cells in the glandular epithelium (enteroendocrine cells); not needed to answer.","tg":["H15A slide 76 (Fundic/Body cells)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 interactive","n":"Digestive system interactive Fall 2026","sl":13,"f":"G017"},{"t":"2024 clicker","n":"W10 Digestive clicker session","sl":9,"f":"F0702"}],"tier":1,"n":"14"}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":9,"label":"Pernicious anemia: no B12, large red cells","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G015-85.jpg","cap":"Pernicious anemia: no parietal-cell intrinsic factor, so B12 deficiency and large red cells","where":"2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 85"},{"src":"../sl/G017-17.jpg","cap":"Smear: hypersegmented neutrophil, oval macrocytes and Howell-Jolly body in B12 deficiency","where":"2026 digestive interactive, slide 17"}],"q":[{"id":"G017-21-1","s":"In pernicious anemia, the parietal cells no longer secrete intrinsic factor. Which vitamin becomes deficient, and what type of anemia results?","o":["A. Folate; microcytic anemia","B. Vitamin B12; megaloblastic (macrocytic) anemia","C. Iron; microcytic hypochromic anemia","D. Vitamin B6; sideroblastic anemia","E. Vitamin B12; microcytic anemia"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Vitamin B12; megaloblastic (macrocytic) anemia","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Vitamin B12; megaloblastic anemia.</b> Parietal cells secrete intrinsic factor, which binds vitamin B12 so the terminal ileum can absorb it. Without it, B12 falls, DNA synthesis in the marrow slows, and red cells come out large (megaloblastic).<br>A. Folate, microcytic: folate is absorbed without intrinsic factor, and its deficiency also makes large, not small, cells.<br>C. Iron, microcytic: low acid can reduce iron absorption, but the missing intrinsic factor is the defining defect.<br>D. Vitamin B6, sideroblastic: B6 is needed for heme synthesis and has nothing to do with intrinsic factor.<br>E. Vitamin B12, microcytic: the vitamin is right, but B12 lack gives large cells.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15A slide 85 (Pernicious anemia)","H15A slide 76 (Fundic/Body cells)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 interactive","n":"Digestive system interactive Fall 2026","sl":21,"f":"G017"}],"tier":1,"n":"15"},{"id":"G017-16-1","s":"A 60-year-old woman with type 1 diabetes and Hashimoto thyroiditis has fatigue, numbness and tingling, and unsteadiness. Hemoglobin is 10 g/dL and MCV (mean corpuscular volume) is 110 fL. Her peripheral blood smear is shown. Which findings does it show?","o":["A. Small pale red cells with central pallor wider than one-third of the cell (iron deficiency)","B. Oval macrocytes, a hypersegmented neutrophil and a Howell-Jolly body (megaloblastic anemia)","C. Sickled red cells and target cells (sickle cell disease)","D. Spherocytes without central pallor (hereditary spherocytosis)","E. Schistocytes (fragmented red cells) from microangiopathic hemolysis"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Oval macrocytes, a hypersegmented neutrophil and a Howell-Jolly body (megaloblastic anemia)","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Oval macrocytes, a hypersegmented neutrophil and a Howell-Jolly body.</b> Big oval red cells, a neutrophil with too many nuclear lobes and a small nuclear leftover inside a red cell mean megaloblastic anemia. With an MCV of 110 fL in a woman with other autoimmune diseases, the cause is vitamin B12 deficiency from pernicious anemia.<br>A. Iron deficiency: small, pale cells and a low MCV.<br>C. Sickle cell disease: curved, sickled cells; it is inherited, not autoimmune.<br>D. Hereditary spherocytosis: small round cells without central pallor and a normal or low MCV.<br>E. Schistocytes: cell fragments from damage in small vessels, as in hemolytic uremic syndrome or thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/G017-16.jpg","in":"Peripheral blood smear with large oval red cells, a neutrophil with many nuclear lobes, and a red cell containing a small dark nuclear remnant (Howell-Jolly body).","tg":["H15A slide 85 (Pernicious anemia)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 interactive","n":"Digestive system interactive Fall 2026","sl":16,"f":"G017"},{"t":"2024 clicker","n":"W10 Digestive clicker session","sl":12,"f":"F0702"}],"tier":1,"n":"16"}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":9,"label":"Pernicious anemia: parietal cells lost","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G015-76.jpg","cap":"Parietal (oxyntic) cells secrete HCl and intrinsic factor for vitamin B12 absorption","where":"2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 76"},{"src":"../sl/G015-85.jpg","cap":"Pernicious anemia: intrinsic factor lost; stomach bypass surgery can also cause it","where":"2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 85"}],"q":[{"id":"G017-19-1","s":"A 60-year-old woman with type 1 diabetes and Hashimoto thyroiditis has megaloblastic anemia from low vitamin B12. Endoscopy shows diffuse atrophic gastritis of the fundus and body; Helicobacter pylori is negative. Which pattern of gastritis and which antibodies fit this autoimmune pernicious anemia?","o":["A. Antrum-predominant gastritis; antibodies to gastrin-secreting G cells","B. Atrophic autoimmune gastritis of the fundus and body; antibodies to the parietal cell H⁺/K⁺-ATPase (proton pump) and to intrinsic factor","C. Helicobacter pylori gastritis of the antrum; antibodies to urease","D. Acute erosive gastritis; antibodies to chief cell pepsinogen","E. Atrophic gastritis of the fundus and body; antibodies to tissue transglutaminase"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Atrophic autoimmune gastritis of the fundus and body; antibodies to the parietal cell H⁺/K⁺-ATPase (proton pump) and to intrinsic factor","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Atrophic autoimmune gastritis; antibodies to the H⁺/K⁺-ATPase and to intrinsic factor.</b> The immune attack targets the fundus and body, where parietal cells live. Losing them means no acid and no intrinsic factor, so vitamin B12 is not absorbed. It travels with other autoimmune diseases such as type 1 diabetes and Hashimoto thyroiditis.<br>A. Antral gastritis with G-cell antibodies: the antrum is spared in autoimmune gastritis, and gastrin rises because acid is low.<br>C. Helicobacter pylori gastritis: mostly antral, with no autoantibodies; she tested negative.<br>D. Acute erosive gastritis: from NSAIDs, alcohol or stress, with no autoantibodies.<br>E. Tissue transglutaminase antibodies: the marker of celiac disease, a small-intestine disease.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15A slide 85 (Pernicious anemia)","H15A slide 76 (Fundic/Body cells)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 interactive","n":"Digestive system interactive Fall 2026","sl":19,"f":"G017"}],"tier":1,"n":"17"},{"id":"G017-23-1","s":"[Case context, slides 16-18: 60-year-old woman with type 1 diabetes and Hashimoto thyroiditis; fatigue, worsening numbness/tingling, unsteadiness, foul-smelling diarrhea, pale conjunctiva, HR 100; Hb 10, MCV 110 fL; low vitamin B12; EGD: diffuse atrophic gastritis of fundus and body, H. pylori negative, autoimmune pattern.] An image from the gastric body biopsy is presented below with an image from a more normal gastric body for comparison. Absence of which cell type in the patient's gastric body is most likely responsible for her clinical findings?","o":["A. Chief cells","B. Parietal cells","C. Surface mucous cells","D. Enteroendocrine cells","E. Mucous neck cells"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Parietal cells","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Parietal cells.</b> Autoimmune atrophic gastritis destroys the <b>parietal cells</b> of the fundus and body. They make <b>intrinsic factor</b>, so vitamin B12 is not absorbed: megaloblastic anemia plus nerve damage (numbness, unsteadiness).<br>A. Chief cells: make pepsinogen; losing them does not cause B12 deficiency.<br>C. Surface mucous cells: protective alkaline mucus.<br>D. Enteroendocrine cells: hormones such as gastrin and serotonin.<br>E. Mucous neck cells: acidic mucus.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"Biopsy (chronic atrophic gastritis): thin mucosa with few, sparse glands and inflamed lamina propria vs normal body mucosa packed with tall oxyntic glands.","tg":["H15A slide 85 (Pernicious anemia)","H15A slide 76 (Fundic/Body cells)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 interactive","n":"Digestive system interactive Fall 2026","sl":23,"f":"G017"},{"t":"2024 clicker","n":"W10 Digestive clicker session","sl":15,"f":"F0702"}],"tier":1,"n":"18"}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":10,"label":"Micrograph: fold with submucosal core = plica","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G016-21.jpg","cap":"Jejunum: prominent plicae circulares (P) with thin submucosal cores (SM)","where":"2026 lecture: H15B Digestive Tract, slide 21","lg":[["V","villi covering each fold"],["P","plica circularis (circular fold)"],["SM","submucosa (blue-green core of each fold)"],["M","muscularis externa (inner circular, outer longitudinal)"],["S","serosa"]]},{"src":"../sl/G021-16.jpg","cap":"Definitions: plica has submucosal core; villus has lamina propria core; microvilli apical","where":"2025 supplemental review, slide 16"}],"q":[{"id":"G018-58-1","s":"Q11. Name the circled structure:","o":["A. Plicae circularis","B. Brush border (microvilli)","C. Villi","D. Rugae"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Plicae circularis","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Plicae circularis.</b> The circle holds a whole fold that has a core of submucosa (connective tissue) and is covered with many villi. Plicae circulares (valves of Kerckring) are permanent circular folds of mucosa plus submucosa: the largest of the three ways the small intestine enlarges its surface (plicae, then villi, then microvilli).<br>B. Brush border (microvilli): far too small to see at this power; it is the striped edge on top of each absorptive cell.<br>C. Villi: the many finger-like projections on the surface of the fold; each has only a lamina propria core.<br>D. Rugae: folds of the stomach wall, which flatten out when the stomach fills.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/G018-58.jpg","in":"Low power small intestine; an oval circles one entire tall fold (with a connective-tissue core) covered by villi.","tg":["H15B slide 11 (Plicae: increases absorption surface.)","H15B slide 10 (Digests food and absorbs end products of digestion.)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 lab","n":"Digestive system lab deck Fall 2026 (practice questions)","sl":58,"f":"G018"}],"tier":1,"lg":[["circle","plica circularis: mucosa fold with a submucosa core, covered by villi"]],"n":"19"}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":10,"label":"Micrograph: finger-like projection = villus","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G018-37.jpg","cap":"Light-microscope level: intestinal villi with lamina propria cores","where":"2026 digestive lab deck, slide 37"},{"src":"../sl/G021-16.jpg","cap":"Villi: finger-like fronds of columnar epithelium around a lamina propria core","where":"2025 supplemental review, slide 16"}],"q":[{"id":"G018-59-1","s":"Q12. Name the circled structure:","o":["A. Plicae circularis","B. MicrovillIi","C. Villus","D. Rugae"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Villus","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Villus.</b> The circle holds one finger-like projection of mucosa: epithelium wrapped around a core of lamina propria, sticking out from the side of a larger fold. Villi are the middle size of the three surface enlargements of the small intestine.<br>A. Plicae circularis: the whole large fold that carries the villi; its core is submucosa.<br>B. Microvilli: tiny projections of the cell membrane, seen only as the brush border at high power.<br>D. Rugae: stomach folds, not found in the small intestine.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/G018-59.jpg","in":"Medium power: one fold with many finger-like projections; an oval (and arrow) on a single projection.","tg":["H15B slide 11 (Plicae: increases absorption surface.)","H15B slide 10 (Digests food and absorbs end products of digestion.)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 lab","n":"Digestive system lab deck Fall 2026 (practice questions)","sl":59,"f":"G018"}],"tier":1,"lg":[["circle and arrow","one villus: finger of lamina propria covered by enterocytes"]],"n":"20"}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":10,"label":"Brush border = microvilli","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G018-39.jpg","cap":"Brush border on enterocytes, and its microvilli by electron microscopy","where":"2026 digestive lab deck, slide 39"}],"q":[{"id":"G018-60-1","s":"Q13. Name the structure shown by the arrow:","o":["A. Plicae circularis","B. Brush border (microvilli)","C. Villi","D. Rugae"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Brush border (microvilli)","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Brush border (microvilli).</b> The arrow points to the thin, pink, finely striped band on the top (apical) surface of the columnar absorptive cells (enterocytes). It is made of thousands of microvilli on each cell and carries the brush-border enzymes for the last steps of digestion.<br>A. Plicae circularis: large folds with a submucosal core, seen at low power.<br>C. Villi: finger-like projections made of many cells, not a band on top of single cells.<br>D. Rugae: folds of the stomach wall.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/G018-60.jpg","in":"High power of a villus tip: arrow on the pink apical border of the columnar cells.","tg":["H15B slide 13 (Columnar absorptive cells (enterocyte))","H15B slide 12 (Mucosa features)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 lab","n":"Digestive system lab deck Fall 2026 (practice questions)","sl":60,"f":"G018"}],"tier":1,"lg":[["arrow","brush border (microvilli) on enterocytes"]],"n":"21"}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":11,"label":"Enterocytes: terminal digestion and absorption","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G018-38.jpg","cap":"Enterocyte microvilli enlarge surface for terminal digestion and absorption","where":"2026 digestive lab deck, slide 38"},{"src":"../sl/G018-39.jpg","cap":"Enterocytes: brush border of microvilli by light and electron microscopy","where":"2026 digestive lab deck, slide 39"}],"q":[{"id":"G017-30-1","s":"A sample of small intestinal mucosa from a patient with celiac disease and a sample of normal small intestinal mucosa for comparison are illustrated below. Given the histologically apparent structural alterations of the small intestinal mucosa seen here (celiac disease: villous atrophy, crypt hyperplasia), what normal functions would you expect to be most abnormal in the patient with celiac disease?","o":["A. Concentration of bile","B. Terminal digestion and nutrient absorption","C. Water resorption","D. Initiation of carbohydrate digestion","E. Initiation of protein and triglyceride digestion"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Terminal digestion and nutrient absorption","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Terminal digestion and nutrient absorption.</b> Celiac disease flattens the villi (<b>villous atrophy</b>) and deepens the crypts. Fewer villi and microvilli mean less surface and fewer <b>brush-border enzymes</b> on enterocytes, so the last steps of digestion and absorption fail.<br>A. Concentrating bile: the gallbladder.<br>C. Water resorption: mostly the colon.<br>D. Starting carbohydrate digestion: salivary amylase in the mouth.<br>E. Starting protein and triglyceride digestion: pepsin and gastric lipase in the stomach.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"Celiac mucosa: flat surface with no villi and long hyperplastic crypts; normal mucosa: tall villi over short crypts (labels in stem).","tg":["H15B slide 11 (Plicae: increases absorption surface.)","H15B slide 13 (Columnar absorptive cells (enterocyte))"],"src":[{"t":"2026 interactive","n":"Digestive system interactive Fall 2026","sl":30,"f":"G017"},{"t":"2024 clicker","n":"W10 Digestive clicker session","sl":24,"f":"F0702"},{"t":"2024 Middletown clicker","n":"lecture clickers week 10 digestive","sl":48,"f":"F3961"}],"tier":1,"n":"22"}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":12,"label":"Paneth granules: lysozyme and defensins","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G018-38.jpg","cap":"Paneth cells: innate immunity; granules release lysozyme, defensins, phospholipase A2","where":"2026 digestive lab deck, slide 38"},{"src":"../sl/G016-18.jpg","cap":"Crypt base: Paneth cells packed with bright red apical granules (arrow)","where":"2026 lecture: H15B Digestive Tract, slide 18","lg":[["arrow","Paneth cell granules (large, bright red, toward the lumen)"],["arrowhead","enteroendocrine cell (pale, finely granular cytoplasm, wedged between crypt cells)"],["M","mast cell in the lamina propria (packed with purple-red granules)"]]}],"q":[{"id":"G017-27-1","s":"The granules within the cells at the pointer are rich in which of the following substances?","o":["A. Defensins","B. Cholecystokinin","C. Pepsin","D. IgA","E. Mucin"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Defensins","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Defensins.</b> Cells at the <b>crypt base</b> packed with <b>bright red (eosinophilic) apical granules</b> are <b>Paneth cells</b>. Their granules release <b>defensins, lysozyme and phospholipase A2</b>, which kill bacteria (innate immunity).<br>B. Cholecystokinin: from enteroendocrine cells.<br>C. Pepsin: made as pepsinogen by stomach chief cells.<br>D. IgA (immunoglobulin A): from plasma cells in the lamina propria.<br>E. Mucin: from goblet cells, which look pale, not red.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/G017-27.jpg","in":"High-power crypt base of small intestine; arrows point to cells packed with bright red/magenta apical granules (Paneth cells).","tg":["H15B slide 13 (Columnar absorptive cells (enterocyte))","H15B slide 17 (Crypts of Lieberkühn)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 interactive","n":"Digestive system interactive Fall 2026","sl":27,"f":"G017"},{"t":"2024 clicker","n":"W10 Digestive clicker session","sl":21,"f":"F0702"},{"t":"2024 Middletown clicker","n":"lecture clickers week 10 digestive","sl":24,"f":"F3961"},{"t":"2023 clicker","n":"CL10 digestive system clickers","sl":17,"f":"F0731"},{"t":"2023 faculty review","n":"Digestive system review 2023","sl":19,"f":"F0744"}],"tier":1,"lg":[["arrows","Paneth cell granules (bright red, crypt base; defensins, lysozyme)"]],"n":"23"}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":12,"label":"Paneth cells: red granules at crypt base","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G016-17.jpg","cap":"Crypt base: Paneth cells with red granules (A); circled cell is dividing","where":"2026 lecture: H15B Digestive Tract, slide 17","lg":[["A (arrowheads)","Paneth cells: big red granules (lysozyme, defensins) at the crypt base"],["circle","dividing cell (mitotic figure: clumped chromosomes with a clear halo); crypts are where new epithelial cells are made"]]},{"src":"../sl/G016-18.jpg","cap":"Paneth cells: crypt-base cells packed with bright red apical granules","where":"2026 lecture: H15B Digestive Tract, slide 18","lg":[["arrow","Paneth cell granules (large, bright red, toward the lumen)"],["arrowhead","enteroendocrine cell (pale, finely granular cytoplasm, wedged between crypt cells)"],["M","mast cell in the lamina propria (packed with purple-red granules)"]]}],"q":[{"id":"G018-55-1","s":"Q8. The granules in the cell indicated by the letter “A” contain:","o":["A. Pepsinogen","B. Intrinsic factor","C. Histamine","D. Gastrin","E. Lysozyme"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Lysozyme","a":"E","why":"<b>E. Lysozyme.</b> Cell A sits at the base of a crypt of Lieberkühn and is packed with large, bright red (eosinophilic) granules: a Paneth cell. Its granules hold antibacterial proteins: lysozyme, defensins and phospholipase A2. (The circled cell is a dividing stem cell.)<br>A. Pepsinogen: chief cells of the stomach.<br>B. Intrinsic factor: parietal cells of the stomach.<br>C. Histamine: enterochromaffin-like (ECL) cells of the stomach.<br>D. Gastrin: G cells of the pyloric antrum.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/G018-55.jpg","in":"Base of an intestinal crypt: cell A filled with large bright red granules; a mitotic figure is circled nearby.","tg":["H15B slide 13 (Columnar absorptive cells (enterocyte))","H15B slide 17 (Crypts of Lieberkühn)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 lab","n":"Digestive system lab deck Fall 2026 (practice questions)","sl":55,"f":"G018"}],"tier":1,"lg":[["A (arrowheads)","Paneth cells: big red granules (lysozyme, defensins) at the crypt base"],["circle","dividing cell (mitotic figure: clumped chromosomes with a clear halo); crypts are where new epithelial cells are made"]],"n":"24"}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":13,"label":"Brunner glands → duodenum","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G016-19.jpg","cap":"Duodenum: Brunner's glands fill the submucosa below the muscularis mucosae","where":"2026 lecture: H15B Digestive Tract, slide 19","lg":[["dashed line","boundary between villi (above) and intestinal crypts (below)"],["arrow","Brunner gland duct crossing the muscularis mucosae into a crypt"]]},{"src":"../sl/G017-25.jpg","cap":"Brunner gland mucus, pH eight to nine-and-a-half, neutralizes acid chyme","where":"2026 digestive interactive, slide 25"}],"q":[{"id":"G017-24-1","s":"Histological evaluation of a portion of the gastrointestinal tract from a 55-year-old man with long-term gastrointestinal complaints shows ramified, tubular glands located in the submucosal layer. The glands contain secretions with pH close to 9.0. Which of the following segments of the gastrointestinal tract matches these findings?","o":["A. Stomach","B. Duodenum","C. Jejunum","D. Ileum","E. Ascending colon"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Duodenum","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Duodenum.</b> Branched tubular glands in the <b>submucosa</b> that make <b>alkaline mucus (pH near nine)</b> are <b>Brunner glands</b>. They neutralize acid chyme from the stomach and, among intestinal segments, exist only in the duodenum.<br>A. Stomach: its glands lie in the mucosa and secrete acid.<br>C. Jejunum: thin submucosa with no glands; tall plicae.<br>D. Ileum: Peyer's patches, no submucosal glands.<br>E. Ascending colon: crypts in the mucosa only.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"Small intestine with villi (mucosa) above and lobules of pale branched glands filling the submucosa (Brunner glands); stem gives the finding.","tg":["H15B slide 9 (Distinguishing features)","H15B slide 20 (MUCOSA)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 interactive","n":"Digestive system interactive Fall 2026","sl":24,"f":"G017"},{"t":"2024 clicker","n":"W10 Digestive clicker session","sl":18,"f":"F0702"},{"t":"2024 Middletown clicker","n":"lecture clickers week 10 digestive","sl":3,"f":"F3961"},{"t":"2023 clicker","n":"CL10 digestive system clickers","sl":2,"f":"F0731"}],"tier":1,"n":"25"}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":13,"label":"Micrograph: submucosal glands → duodenum","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G016-19.jpg","cap":"Duodenum: Brunner's glands fill the submucosa below the muscularis mucosae","where":"2026 lecture: H15B Digestive Tract, slide 19","lg":[["dashed line","boundary between villi (above) and intestinal crypts (below)"],["arrow","Brunner gland duct crossing the muscularis mucosae into a crypt"]]},{"src":"../sl/G018-36.jpg","cap":"Three segments: duodenum Brunner glands, jejunum plicae, ileum Peyer's patches","where":"2026 digestive lab deck, slide 36","lg":[["dashed line","border between villi (above) and crypts (below)"],["arrow","Brunner gland duct emptying into the base of a crypt"],["P","plica circularis (fold with a submucosa core)"],["SM","submucosa"],["M","muscularis externa"],["large box","one Peyer patch lymphoid nodule with the mucosa over it"],["small box","edge of a lymphoid nodule against the submucosa"]]}],"q":[{"id":"G018-56-1","s":"Q9. Which organ is depicted below?","o":["A. Stomach","B. Duodenum","C. Jejunum","D. Ileum","E. Colon"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Duodenum","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Duodenum.</b> The surface has villi, so this is small intestine, and the submucosa is filled with a thick layer of pale mucous glands: the Brunner (submucosal) glands. Brunner glands are found only in the duodenum; their alkaline mucus helps neutralize the acid coming from the stomach.<br>A. Stomach: gastric pits and glands, no villi.<br>C. Jejunum: tall plicae and villi, but no glands in the submucosa.<br>D. Ileum: Peyer patches (large lymphoid nodules), no submucosal glands.<br>E. Colon: no villi; straight crypts full of goblet cells.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/G018-56.jpg","in":"Low power: villous mucosa with a broad band of pale glands below the muscularis mucosae, then muscularis externa.","tg":["H15B slide 9 (Distinguishing features)","H15B slide 20 (MUCOSA)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 lab","n":"Digestive system lab deck Fall 2026 (practice questions)","sl":56,"f":"G018"}],"tier":1,"n":"26"}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":13,"label":"Micrograph: tall plicae, no glands → jejunum","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G016-21.jpg","cap":"Jejunum: prominent plicae circulares, thin non-remarkable submucosa","where":"2026 lecture: H15B Digestive Tract, slide 21","lg":[["V","villi covering each fold"],["P","plica circularis (circular fold)"],["SM","submucosa (blue-green core of each fold)"],["M","muscularis externa (inner circular, outer longitudinal)"],["S","serosa"]]},{"src":"../sl/G018-36.jpg","cap":"Three segments: duodenum Brunner glands, jejunum prominent plicae, ileum Peyer's patches","where":"2026 digestive lab deck, slide 36","lg":[["dashed line","border between villi (above) and crypts (below)"],["arrow","Brunner gland duct emptying into the base of a crypt"],["P","plica circularis (fold with a submucosa core)"],["SM","submucosa"],["M","muscularis externa"],["large box","one Peyer patch lymphoid nodule with the mucosa over it"],["small box","edge of a lymphoid nodule against the submucosa"]]}],"q":[{"id":"G018-57-1","s":"Q10. Which organ is depicted below?","o":["A. Stomach","B. Duodenum","C. Jejunum","D. Ileum","E. Colon"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Jejunum","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Jejunum.</b> Tall, well-developed plicae circulares (folds with a submucosal core), each covered with villi. The submucosa is thin and has no glands, and the lymphoid nodules are small. Plicae are most developed in the jejunum.<br>A. Stomach: rugae and gastric pits, no villi.<br>B. Duodenum: would show Brunner glands in the submucosa.<br>D. Ileum: would show large Peyer patches; its plicae are smaller.<br>E. Colon: no villi or plicae; straight crypts with goblet cells.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/G018-57.jpg","in":"Low power: several tall folds of mucosa with a submucosal core, each covered by villi.","tg":["H15B slide 21 (Prominent plicae circulares)","H15B slide 22 (MUCOSA)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 lab","n":"Digestive system lab deck Fall 2026 (practice questions)","sl":57,"f":"G018"}],"tier":1,"n":"27"}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":14,"label":"Micrograph: crypts, goblet cells, no villi → colon","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G016-28.jpg","cap":"Colon mucosa: flat surface, straight glands (Gl) full of goblet cells, no villi","where":"2026 lecture: H15B Digestive Tract, slide 28","lg":[["arrows","gland openings on the flat surface"],["box (upper right)","surface epithelium and gland tops"],["Gl","straight intestinal glands (crypts), full of goblet cells"],["LP","lamina propria"],["box (lower left)","gland bases"],["Muc","mucosa"],["SubM","submucosa"],["MM","muscularis mucosae"]]},{"src":"../sl/G016-27.jpg","cap":"Colon mucosa: straight crypts packed with goblet cells, lymphoid tissue below","where":"2026 lecture: H15B Digestive Tract, slide 27"}],"q":[{"id":"G018-61-1","s":"Q14. Which organ is depicted below?","o":["A. Stomach","B. Duodenum","C. Ileum","D. Colon","E. Jejunum"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Colon","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Colon.</b> The surface is flat with no villi, and the mucosa is packed with straight tubular crypts full of pale goblet cells. That pattern is the large intestine, whose main jobs are absorbing water and making mucus.<br>A. Stomach: gastric pits leading into glands with parietal and chief cells, few goblet cells.<br>B. Duodenum: villi plus Brunner glands in the submucosa.<br>C. Ileum: villi plus Peyer patches.<br>E. Jejunum: villi on tall plicae.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/G018-61.jpg","in":"Mucosa of straight tubular glands crowded with pale goblet cells; flat surface; muscularis mucosae and submucosa below.","tg":["H15B slide 9 (Distinguishing features)","H15B slide 27 (Colon)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 lab","n":"Digestive system lab deck Fall 2026 (practice questions)","sl":61,"f":"G018"}],"tier":1,"n":"28"}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":15,"label":"Teniae coli: colon's outer longitudinal muscle","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G016-26.jpg","cap":"Large intestine tunics: outer longitudinal muscularis forms the tenia coli","where":"2026 lecture: H15B Digestive Tract, slide 26"},{"src":"../sl/G018-43.jpg","cap":"Large intestine: outer muscularis in three longitudinal ribbons, the taenia coli","where":"2026 digestive lab deck, slide 43"}],"q":[{"id":"G017-33-1","s":"In the gastrointestinal tract, the muscularis propria consists of an inner circular layer and an outer longitudinal layer of smooth muscle with an intervening neural plexus (Auerbach). Exceptions to this general plan are seen in the stomach where an inner oblique layer of smooth muscle is added and in a second location where the outer layer is condensed into three longitudinal ribbons (taenia) of smooth muscle. Where can we expect to find this modified organization and what plane of section would be required to demonstrate it?","o":["A. Esophagus, longitudinal section","B. Ileum, transverse section","C. Colon, longitudinal section","D. Colon, transverse section","E. Anal canal, external sphincter, transverse section"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Colon, transverse section","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Colon, transverse section.</b> In the colon, the outer longitudinal muscle gathers into <b>three bands, the teniae coli</b>. A <b>cross (transverse) section</b> shows all three as separate thick bundles around the wall, with thin muscle between them.<br>A. Esophagus: complete outer longitudinal layer, no teniae.<br>B. Ileum: complete outer longitudinal layer.<br>C. Colon, longitudinal section: right organ, but a lengthwise cut shows at most one strip, not three bands.<br>E. External anal sphincter: a ring of skeletal muscle, not teniae.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"Eroschenko drawing of a transverse section of colon wall with intestinal glands, muscularis externa and a boxed label 'Taeniae coli' pointing at the thickened outer longitudinal band.","tg":["H15B slide 30 (Reabsorption:)","H15B slide 34 (Appendix)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 interactive","n":"Digestive system interactive Fall 2026","sl":33,"f":"G017"},{"t":"2024 clicker","n":"W10 Digestive clicker session","sl":25,"f":"F0702"}],"tier":1,"n":"29"}]}],"sum":{},"units":[{"l":"HIST-11","h":"The lingual papillae","p":"<p>Papillae are raised folds of the epithelium, each with a core of connective tissue, on the dorsal surface of the anterior two-thirds of the tongue. There are <b>four types</b>.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G018-17.jpg\" alt=\"The four papillae: filiform, foliate, fungiform, circumvallate\"><figcaption>The four papillae: filiform, foliate, fungiform, circumvallate <small>· 2026 digestive lab deck, slide 17</small></figcaption><div class=\"lgd\"><span class=\"lgl\">Labels</span><b>(c) arrows</b> deep clefts between the foliate ridges<span class=\"sep\">·</span><b>(b) arrows</b> secondary connective tissue papillae pushing up into the epithelium</div></figure>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<tr><th>Type</th><th>Shape and location</th><th>Keratin</th><th>Taste buds</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Filiform</b></td><td>The most numerous. Slender, pointed cones in rows over the whole dorsal surface. They give the tongue its rough texture and grip food (friction) during chewing.</td><td>Heavily keratinized tips</td><td><b>None</b></td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Fungiform</b></td><td>Mushroom-shaped: narrow stalk, broad round top with a connective tissue core. Scattered singly among the filiform papillae, more near the tip.</td><td>Thin</td><td>A few, on the upper surface</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Circumvallate</b> (vallate)</td><td>The largest: 8–12 domes in a V just in front of the sulcus terminalis. Each sits level with the surface, ringed by a deep trench (a moat).</td><td>None</td><td>Many, in the epithelium of the side walls facing the trench</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Foliate</b></td><td>Rows of parallel, leaf-like ridges on the sides of the back of the tongue, separated by deep clefts. Well developed in children, small in adults.</td><td>None</td><td>Many, in the walls of the clefts</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<h4>Von Ebner glands</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Small <b>serous</b> (watery) salivary glands in the tongue under the circumvallate papillae.</li>\n<li>Their ducts empty into the base of the trench. The steady flow rinses the trench, clearing old food so the taste buds can sense new tastes.</li>\n<li>They also secrete lingual lipase.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>On a slide</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Rows of pointed, keratinized spikes: filiform.</li>\n<li>One broad mushroom among spikes: fungiform.</li>\n<li>One large dome in a trench, with glands below: circumvallate.</li>\n<li>A row of parallel ridges with clefts: foliate.</li>\n</ul>","s":"The oral cavity","sn":2,"pn":"2.4"},{"l":"HIST-11","h":"Taste buds","p":"<p>A <b>taste bud</b> is a pale, oval cluster of spindle-shaped cells set inside the stratified epithelium. It reaches from the basal lamina to the surface, where it opens through a small <b>taste pore</b> (gustatory pore).</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G015-36.jpg\" alt=\"Taste bud: gustatory pore, taste cells with microvilli, supporting and basal cells\"><figcaption>Taste bud: gustatory pore, taste cells with microvilli, supporting and basal cells <small>· 2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 36</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Where taste buds are</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Side walls of the circumvallate papillae: the most, about 250 per papilla.</li>\n<li>Walls of the foliate clefts, and the tops of fungiform papillae (a few).</li>\n<li>None on the filiform papillae.</li>\n<li>Also scattered in the soft palate, pharynx and epiglottis.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Cells of a taste bud</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Gustatory (taste, sensory) cells</b>: long, pale cells. Their apical <b>microvilli</b> (not cilia, not stereocilia) reach into the taste pore. There, dissolved substances bind receptors on the microvilli. At their base the cells synapse with afferent nerve fibers.</li>\n<li><b>Supporting (sustentacular) cells</b>: long cells between the taste cells.</li>\n<li><b>Basal cells</b>: small stem cells at the base of the bud. They replace the taste cells, which live only about 10 days.</li>\n<li>Afferent (sensory) nerve fibers enter the bud at its base.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Tastes</h4>\n<p>Salty, sweet, bitter, sour and umami (the taste of glutamate). Water, calcium and fat may be extra tastes (lecture slide 30).</p>","s":"The oral cavity","sn":2,"pn":"2.5"},{"l":"HIST-11","h":"Lining, glands and outer coat","p":"<p>The esophagus is a muscular tube about 25 cm (10 inches) long. It carries food from the pharynx to the stomach. An upper and a lower esophageal sphincter close its two ends. Its wall follows the four-layer plan.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G018-23.jpg\" alt=\"Esophagus: epithelium, lamina propria, muscularis mucosae, and mucous glands in the submucosa\"><figcaption>Esophagus: epithelium, lamina propria, muscularis mucosae, and mucous glands in the submucosa <small>· 2026 digestive lab deck, slide 23</small></figcaption><div class=\"lgd\"><span class=\"lgl\">Labels</span><b>SS</b> stratified squamous epithelium, nonkeratinized<span class=\"sep\">·</span><b>LP</b> lamina propria<span class=\"sep\">·</span><b>MM</b> muscularis mucosae (smooth muscle)<span class=\"sep\">·</span><b>D</b> ducts of the esophageal glands<span class=\"sep\">·</span><b>GL</b> esophageal mucous glands in the submucosa</div></figure>\n<h4>Mucosa</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Epithelium: thick <b>nonkeratinized stratified squamous</b>. The surface cells keep their nuclei. It resists the rubbing of food. Like all stratified squamous linings, it has no goblet cells.</li>\n<li>Lamina propria: loose connective tissue. Near the stomach it holds small mucous glands, the esophageal cardiac glands.</li>\n<li>Muscularis mucosae: a single layer of longitudinal smooth muscle (incomplete near the top).</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Submucosa</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Esophageal glands</b>: mucous glands that lubricate the lining. Their ducts cross the mucosa to the lumen.</li>\n<li>A rich plexus of veins. When pressure in the portal vein rises (portal hypertension, for example from alcoholic cirrhosis), these submucosal veins swell into <b>esophageal varices</b>. They can burst and cause vomiting of blood.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Muscularis externa</h4>\n<p>Inner circular and outer longitudinal layers. The type of muscle changes along the length (3.2).</p>\n<h4>Outer coat</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Adventitia along most of its length, in the neck and chest.</li>\n<li>Serosa only on the short segment below the diaphragm (the last 1–2 cm), which lies in the peritoneal cavity.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Recognizing esophagus on a slide</h4>\n<p>Thick nonkeratinized stratified squamous epithelium, mucous glands in the submucosa, and a thick muscularis externa.</p>\n<h4>Infection of the lining</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>In people with weak immunity (for example AIDS, acquired immunodeficiency syndrome), the yeast Candida can grow on the lining. It forms raised <b>white plaques</b> on red, swollen mucosa and makes swallowing painful.</li>\n<li>Herpes simplex virus instead makes small punched-out ulcers.</li>\n</ul>","s":"The esophagus","sn":3,"pn":"3.1"},{"l":"HIST-11","h":"The muscle changes from top to bottom","p":"<p>The esophagus is divided into thirds by the type of muscle in its <b>muscularis externa</b>.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G015-60.jpg\" alt=\"Esophagus: skeletal, mixed and smooth muscle by thirds\"><figcaption>Esophagus: skeletal, mixed and smooth muscle by thirds <small>· 2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 60</small></figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<tr><th>Third</th><th>Muscularis externa</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Upper</td><td>Skeletal muscle (swallowing starts as a voluntary act)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Middle</td><td>Skeletal and smooth muscle mixed</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Lower</td><td>Smooth muscle only, like the rest of the gut</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>The esophagus is the <b>only part of the digestive tube with skeletal muscle in its muscularis externa</b>. (The external anal sphincter is skeletal too, but it is a separate sphincter muscle.)</li>\n<li>Skeletal fibers: large, pink, cross-striated, with many nuclei at the edge of each fiber.</li>\n<li>Smooth muscle cells: small spindles with one central nucleus.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Reading a cross-section of the tube</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>The inner circular layer is cut lengthwise, so its cells look long.</li>\n<li>The outer longitudinal layer is cut across, so its fibers look like round or polygonal profiles.</li>\n<li>The myenteric plexus sits in the thin connective tissue between them.</li>\n<li>Do not confuse the muscularis externa with the muscularis mucosae, the thin band of smooth muscle right under the epithelium.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Lower esophageal sphincter and achalasia</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>At the lower end, the circular muscle stays contracted as the <b>lower esophageal sphincter</b>. During swallowing, nerve cells of the myenteric plexus make it relax.</li>\n<li>In <b>achalasia</b>, the myenteric plexus nerve cells at the sphincter are lost. The sphincter cannot relax. Food piles up, and the esophagus above it widens (a bird's-beak shape on a contrast X-ray). Swallowing of both solids and liquids gets harder.</li>\n</ul>","s":"The esophagus","sn":3,"pn":"3.2"},{"l":"HIST-11","h":"Reflux, esophagitis and metaplasia","p":"<h4>Gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD)</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Acid from the stomach flows back into the lower esophagus when the lower esophageal sphincter does not close well.</li>\n<li>Triggers: large meals, lying down after eating, obesity, coffee, alcohol, smoking.</li>\n<li>Symptoms: heartburn (burning pain behind the breastbone) and a sour taste, eased by antacids.</li>\n<li>Treatment starts with lifestyle changes (smaller meals, no eating before lying down, weight loss, less coffee and alcohol), then acid-lowering drugs (proton pump inhibitors).</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Reflux esophagitis on a biopsy</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Basal cell hyperplasia: the basal layer of the squamous epithelium thickens.</li>\n<li>The lamina propria papillae stretch up toward the surface.</li>\n<li>Inflammatory cells (eosinophils, neutrophils, lymphocytes) appear inside the epithelium.</li>\n<li>Other causes of esophagitis to rule out: eosinophilic (allergic) esophagitis, infections (Candida, herpes, cytomegalovirus), pills that stick in the esophagus, and radiation.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Metaplasia</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Metaplasia</b> is the replacement of one mature cell type by another mature type that better withstands a chronic injury. It can reverse if the injury stops.</li>\n<li>Example in the airway: in smokers, ciliated pseudostratified epithelium becomes stratified squamous.</li>\n<li>Compare: dysplasia is disordered growth of abnormal cells (a precancer); neoplasia is new, uncontrolled growth (a tumor).</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Barrett esophagus</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Years of acid reflux turn the stratified squamous lining of the <b>distal esophagus</b>, just above the esophagogastric junction, into <b>simple columnar epithelium with goblet cells</b> (intestinal-type metaplasia). Columnar cells resist acid better.</li>\n<li>The normal esophagus is squamous all the way down to the junction.</li>\n</ul>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G015-66.jpg\" alt=\"Normal esophagus (left) and Barrett esophagus (right)\"><figcaption>Normal esophagus (left) and Barrett esophagus (right) <small>· 2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 66</small></figcaption></figure>","s":"The esophagus","sn":3,"pn":"3.4"},{"l":"HIST-11","h":"Barrett esophagus in the clinic, and cancer","p":"<h4>Recognizing Barrett esophagus</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Story: long-standing heartburn, worse after meals or lying down.</li>\n<li>Endoscopy: <b>salmon-pink or red, velvety mucosa</b> reaching up from the junction into the lower esophagus. Normal squamous lining is pale pinkish-white.</li>\n<li>Biopsy: columnar cells <b>with goblet cells</b> where squamous epithelium should be.</li>\n<li>Cause: the esophagus is exposed to refluxed gastric acid (and bile) for years. It is not caused by Helicobacter pylori, and it is not a motility problem.</li>\n<li>Goblet cells are the key. Stomach mucosa (including the cardia) has no goblet cells. So columnar mucosa with goblet cells in the esophagus is intestinal metaplasia, not misplaced stomach lining.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Where Barrett can lead</h4>\n<p>Barrett esophagus → low-grade dysplasia → high-grade dysplasia → invasive <b>adenocarcinoma</b>, a cancer of gland-forming columnar cells.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G015-65.jpg\" alt=\"Normal lining → Barrett → dysplasia → invasive carcinoma\"><figcaption>Normal lining → Barrett → dysplasia → invasive carcinoma <small>· 2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 65</small></figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<tr><th></th><th>Adenocarcinoma</th><th>Squamous cell carcinoma</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Starts from</td><td>Barrett (columnar) epithelium</td><td>The normal squamous epithelium</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Where</td><td>Lower third</td><td>Upper and middle thirds</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Main risk factors</td><td>Chronic reflux and Barrett esophagus, obesity, smoking</td><td>Smoking, alcohol</td></tr>\n</table></div>","s":"The esophagus","sn":3,"pn":"3.5"},{"l":"HIST-11","h":"The overall design of the stomach","p":"<ul>\n<li>The widest part of the tube; it holds about 1.5 L.</li>\n<li>It mixes food with acid and enzymes into a soupy paste called chyme.</li>\n<li>Four regions: cardia, fundus, body and pylorus.</li>\n</ul>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G018-29.jpg\" alt=\"Stomach wall: rugae, gastric pits and glands, muscularis mucosae, submucosa, muscularis externa, serosa\"><figcaption>Stomach wall: rugae, gastric pits and glands, muscularis mucosae, submucosa, muscularis externa, serosa <small>· 2026 digestive lab deck, slide 29</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Special features</h4>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<tr><th>Feature</th><th>What it is</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Rugae</b></td><td>Large folds of mucosa with a core of submucosa. They flatten as the stomach fills. Only the stomach has them. (The small intestine has permanent circular folds instead, 5.1.)</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Gastric pits</b></td><td>Funnel-shaped dips of the surface epithelium into the mucosa. The gastric glands open into the bottom of the pits.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Gastric glands</b></td><td>Tubular glands in the lamina propria that secrete the gastric juice.</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<h4>The wall, layer by layer</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Mucosa: simple columnar epithelium of <b>surface mucous cells</b>. Each cell has a pale cap of mucus at its top. There are no goblet cells in the stomach. The lamina propria is squeezed into thin strips between the packed glands. Then a muscularis mucosae.</li>\n<li>Submucosa: connective tissue; it forms the core of the rugae.</li>\n<li>Muscularis externa: <b>three layers</b>: inner oblique, middle circular and outer longitudinal. The extra layer helps churn food.</li>\n<li>Serosa (not adventitia): the stomach hangs in the peritoneal cavity.</li>\n</ul>","s":"The stomach","sn":4,"pn":"4.1"},{"l":"HIST-11","h":"Gland cells of the body and fundus","p":"<p>A gland of the body or fundus has three regions: the pit at the top, a narrow neck (isthmus), and the long body and base. Each region has its own cells.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G018-30.jpg\" alt=\"Fundic mucosa: pits, neck and glands; surface mucous, mucous neck, parietal and chief cells\"><figcaption>Fundic mucosa: pits, neck and glands; surface mucous, mucous neck, parietal and chief cells <small>· 2026 digestive lab deck, slide 30</small></figcaption><div class=\"lgd\"><span class=\"lgl\">Labels</span><b>GP</b> gastric pits<span class=\"sep\">·</span><b>SM</b> surface mucous cells<span class=\"sep\">·</span><b>MN</b> mucous neck cells<span class=\"sep\">·</span><b>P</b> parietal cells (big, pink, round &#x27;fried egg&#x27;)<span class=\"sep\">·</span><b>C</b> chief cells (purple, basophilic, deep in the glands)<span class=\"sep\">·</span><b>MM</b> muscularis mucosae<span class=\"sep\">·</span><b>boxes</b> areas enlarged at right</div></figure>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<tr><th>Cell</th><th>Where</th><th>Look on H&amp;E</th><th>Secretes</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Surface mucous cell</td><td>Surface and pits</td><td>Tall columnar, pale mucus cap on top</td><td>Thick, sticky, <b>alkaline</b> mucus that clings to the surface and protects it from acid</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Mucous neck cell</td><td>Neck</td><td>Smaller, pale</td><td>Watery, less alkaline (more acidic) mucus</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Stem cell</td><td>Neck (isthmus)</td><td>Small, dividing</td><td>Nothing; it replaces the other cells</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Parietal (oxyntic) cell</b></td><td>Mostly the upper half of the gland</td><td>Large, round, <b>pink (acidophilic)</b>, central nucleus; packed with mitochondria</td><td><b>Hydrochloric acid (HCl)</b> and <b>intrinsic factor</b>, which the ileum needs to absorb vitamin B12</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Chief (zymogenic) cell</b></td><td>Base of the gland</td><td><b>Purple-blue (basophilic)</b> from rough endoplasmic reticulum; zymogen granules at the top</td><td><b>Pepsinogen</b> (becomes pepsin in acid; digests protein) and a lipase precursor (gastric lipase; starts fat digestion)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Enteroendocrine cell</td><td>Scattered in the glands</td><td>Pale; needs special stains (4.4)</td><td>Hormones and local signals</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p>Starch digestion is not a stomach job: amylase comes from the salivary glands and the pancreas.</p>","s":"The stomach","sn":4,"pn":"4.3"},{"l":"HIST-11","h":"Enteroendocrine cells, and the parietal cell at work","p":"<h4>Enteroendocrine cells</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Small cells scattered among the gland cells, from the stomach to the colon. Their hormone granules sit at the <b>base</b> of the cell. They release into the blood (endocrine) or onto nearby cells (paracrine), not into the lumen.</li>\n<li>On hematoxylin and eosin (H&amp;E) they look pale and are easy to miss. They are shown with <b>special stains</b>: chromium salts (enterochromaffin cells), silver salts (argentaffin cells), or <b>immunostaining</b> (immunohistochemistry) for their hormones.</li>\n<li>Open type: reaches the lumen with microvilli that sense nutrients, much like taste cells. Closed type: does not reach the lumen.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<tr><th>Cell</th><th>Product</th><th>Effect</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Enterochromaffin (EC) cell</td><td>Serotonin</td><td>Increases gut motility</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Enterochromaffin-like cell</td><td>Histamine (paracrine)</td><td>Increases acid secretion by parietal cells</td></tr>\n<tr><td>G cell (pylorus)</td><td>Gastrin</td><td>Increases acid secretion and motility</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Small-intestine cells</td><td>Cholecystokinin, secretin and others</td><td>See 5.3</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<h4>The parietal cell at rest and at work</h4>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G018-33.jpg\" alt=\"Parietal cell, resting and active\"><figcaption>Parietal cell, resting and active <small>· 2026 digestive lab deck, slide 33</small></figcaption></figure>\n<ul>\n<li>Resting: many tubulovesicles near the top, full of proton pumps; short intracellular canaliculi; few microvilli.</li>\n<li>Active: the tubulovesicles fuse with the cell membrane. They form deep <b>intracellular canaliculi</b> lined with many microvilli. This greatly enlarges the membrane that carries the H+/K+ pumps (proton pumps). Many mitochondria supply the energy.</li>\n</ul>","s":"The stomach","sn":4,"pn":"4.4"},{"l":"HIST-11","h":"Diseases of the stomach","p":"<h4>Pernicious anemia: the chain of events</h4>\n<ol>\n<li>Parietal cells are destroyed or bypassed.</li>\n<li>No <b>intrinsic factor</b> is made. Intrinsic factor normally binds vitamin B12 so the ileum can absorb it.</li>\n<li>Vitamin B12 deficiency follows.</li>\n<li>Red cell precursors cannot make DNA normally, so they grow large: <b>megaloblastic (macrocytic) anemia</b>. The blood smear shows large oval red cells and hypersegmented neutrophils (6 or more nuclear lobes).</li>\n<li>Other effects: fatigue, a smooth red tongue, diarrhea, and nerve damage (numbness, tingling, poor balance).</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Size clue: lack of B12 or folate gives big red cells; lack of iron gives small red cells.</p>\n<h4>Two causes</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Autoimmune atrophic gastritis</b> (true pernicious anemia): antibodies attack the parietal cells (their H+/K+ proton pump) and intrinsic factor. The mucosa of the body and fundus thins. It often comes with other autoimmune diseases, such as type 1 diabetes and Hashimoto thyroiditis.</li>\n<li><b>Stomach bypass surgery</b>: food skips most of the stomach, so the parietal cells no longer supply intrinsic factor. The lecture lists bypass as a cause of pernicious anemia.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Helicobacter pylori</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Curved, rod-shaped bacteria that live in the surface mucus.</li>\n<li>The usual cause of chronic gastritis and <b>peptic ulcer disease</b>.</li>\n<li>Long-term infection builds lymphoid tissue in the mucosa and raises the risk of <b>gastric lymphoma</b> (MALT lymphoma) and of gastric adenocarcinoma.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>A smooth muscle tumor</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>A <b>leiomyoma</b> is a benign tumor of smooth muscle, usually from the muscularis externa. It forms a small, well-defined mass under the mucosa, made of bland spindle cells in bundles that stain for smooth muscle markers (desmin, smooth muscle actin).</li>\n<li>Its look-alike, the gastrointestinal stromal tumor (GIST), comes from the pacemaker cells of the gut wall (interstitial cells of Cajal) and stains for KIT (CD117).</li>\n</ul>","s":"The stomach","sn":4,"pn":"4.5"},{"l":"HIST-11","h":"Three levels of folding, and the crypts","p":"<p>The small intestine (duodenum, jejunum, ileum) finishes digestion, absorbs the products, and releases hormones. Its design is all about <b>surface area</b>. Three levels of folding multiply the absorbing surface.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G018-37.jpg\" alt=\"Plicae circulares, villi and microvilli\"><figcaption>Plicae circulares, villi and microvilli <small>· 2026 digestive lab deck, slide 37</small></figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<tr><th>Level</th><th>Structure</th><th>Core</th><th>How it looks</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Gross</td><td><b>Plicae circulares</b> (valves of Kerckring): permanent circular folds</td><td><b>Submucosa</b></td><td>Tall folds of the whole mucosa, each covered with many villi</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Light microscope</td><td><b>Villi</b>: finger-like or leaf-like projections of the mucosa</td><td><b>Lamina propria</b>, with capillaries, a central lacteal and strands of smooth muscle</td><td>Small projections, each covered by one layer of columnar cells</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Electron microscope</td><td><b>Microvilli</b> on the top of each absorptive cell</td><td>Actin filaments</td><td>Together they form the brush (striated) border, a thin fuzzy pink line on the cell tops</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>Plicae circulares start in the duodenum, are tallest and most numerous in the jejunum, and fade out in the distal ileum. They do not flatten, unlike the rugae of the stomach.</li>\n<li>The number of villi and microvilli falls along the way: duodenum &gt; jejunum &gt; ileum.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Crypts of Lieberkühn (intestinal glands)</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Simple tubular glands. The epithelium dips down between the villi as far as the muscularis mucosae. So they lie in the <b>mucosa</b>, not the submucosa.</li>\n<li>Present along the whole small intestine and the large intestine. (The colon keeps its crypts but has no villi.) The esophagus has none, and the stomach has gastric glands instead.</li>\n<li>They hold the stem cells, Paneth cells and many enteroendocrine cells (5.3).</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>On a slide</h4>\n<p>Villi project above the mucosa. The crypts are the straight tubes below the villi, just above the muscularis mucosae. Glands below the muscularis mucosae, in the submucosa, are something else: the Brunner glands of the duodenum (5.4).</p>","s":"The small intestine","sn":5,"pn":"5.1"},{"l":"HIST-11","h":"Enterocytes and fat absorption","p":"<h4>Cells of the intestinal lining</h4>\n<p>Five cell types line the villi and crypts: enterocytes (by far the most numerous), goblet cells, Paneth cells, enteroendocrine cells and stem cells. This part covers the first two; 5.3 covers the rest.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G018-38.jpg\" alt=\"Small intestine cell types\"><figcaption>Small intestine cell types <small>· 2026 digestive lab deck, slide 38</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Enterocytes (columnar absorptive cells)</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Tall columnar cells with a nucleus near the base and a dense <b>brush border of microvilli</b> on top.</li>\n<li>Enzymes in the brush border (for sugars and short peptides) do the last step of digestion right at the cell surface (<b>terminal digestion</b>). Then the cell absorbs the products.</li>\n<li>Tight junctions seal the cells together, so absorbed material passes through the cells.</li>\n<li>Life cycle: stem cells in the crypts divide; the new cells climb the villus and are shed at its tip after about 5 days. Cells do not divide on the villus itself.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>How enterocytes absorb fat</h4>\n<ol>\n<li>In the lumen, pancreatic lipase splits triglycerides into fatty acids and monoglycerides. Bile salts carry them in tiny droplets (micelles).</li>\n<li>These diffuse into the enterocyte (no active transport is needed).</li>\n<li>In the smooth endoplasmic reticulum, they are rebuilt into <b>triglycerides</b>.</li>\n<li>Proteins are added (rough endoplasmic reticulum, Golgi), packaging them as <b>chylomicrons</b>.</li>\n<li>Chylomicrons leave the base of the cell and enter the <b>lacteal</b>, the lymph capillary in the villus core, not the blood capillaries. They reach the blood later through the thoracic duct.</li>\n</ol>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G018-40.jpg\" alt=\"Lipid absorption and processing by enterocytes\"><figcaption>Lipid absorption and processing by enterocytes <small>· 2026 digestive lab deck, slide 40</small></figcaption><div class=\"lgd\"><span class=\"lgl\">Labels</span><b>SER</b> smooth endoplasmic reticulum (packed with small lipid droplets in a)<span class=\"sep\">·</span><b>(a) arrows</b> lipid globules leaving the cell into the space between cells<span class=\"sep\">·</span><b>RER</b> rough endoplasmic reticulum<span class=\"sep\">·</span><b>1</b> bile acids and lipase split fats into glycerol, fatty acids, monoglycerides<span class=\"sep\">·</span><b>2</b> triglycerides remade in the smooth endoplasmic reticulum<span class=\"sep\">·</span><b>3</b> triglyceride + protein coat = chylomicron<span class=\"sep\">·</span><b>4</b> chylomicrons enter the lacteal (lymph)</div></figure>\n<h4>Goblet cells</h4>\n<p>Scattered among the enterocytes. They secrete mucins, which form the protective mucus layer. Their number rises along the gut: duodenum &lt; jejunum &lt; ileum &lt; colon.</p>\n<h4>Celiac disease</h4>\n<p>An immune reaction to gluten flattens the villi (villous atrophy), deepens the crypts, and fills the epithelium with lymphocytes. With fewer enterocytes and less brush border, nutrients are poorly absorbed: chronic diarrhea and weight loss.</p>","s":"The small intestine","sn":5,"pn":"5.2"},{"l":"HIST-11","h":"Crypt cells: stem cells, Paneth cells and hormone cells","p":"<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G016-17.jpg\" alt=\"Paneth cells at the base of a crypt\"><figcaption>Paneth cells at the base of a crypt <small>· 2026 lecture: H15B Digestive Tract, slide 17</small></figcaption><div class=\"lgd\"><span class=\"lgl\">Labels</span><b>A (arrowheads)</b> Paneth cells: big red granules (lysozyme, defensins) at the crypt base<span class=\"sep\">·</span><b>circle</b> dividing cell (mitotic figure: clumped chromosomes with a clear halo); crypts are where new epithelial cells are made</div></figure>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<tr><th>Cell</th><th>Where</th><th>Look</th><th>Job</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Stem (regenerative) cells</td><td>Lower part of the crypt</td><td>Small; dividing cells (mitotic figures) are often seen</td><td>Renew the whole lining every few days: mostly enterocytes, plus goblet, Paneth and enteroendocrine cells</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Paneth cells</b></td><td><b>Base of the crypts</b></td><td>Pyramidal cells packed with large, <b>bright red (eosinophilic) granules</b> at the top</td><td><b>Innate immunity</b>: release lysozyme, defensins and phospholipase A2, which kill bacteria. This keeps the crypt and its stem cells free of microbes and controls the number of gut bacteria.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Enteroendocrine cells</td><td>Crypts and villi</td><td>Pale; granules at the base; special stains</td><td>Hormones (below)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Goblet cells</td><td>Crypts and villi</td><td>Pale mucus-filled cup; nucleus at the base</td><td>Mucus</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p>Do not confuse the red apical granules of Paneth cells with the stomach's chief cells (purple, in gastric glands) or with enteroendocrine cells (pale, granules at the base).</p>\n<h4>Hormones of the small intestine</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Cholecystokinin (CCK)</b>: released when fat and protein reach the duodenum. It makes the gallbladder contract (bile release) and the pancreatic acinar cells secrete enzymes. It also slows the stomach and signals fullness.</li>\n<li><b>Secretin</b>: released when acid reaches the duodenum. It makes the pancreatic ducts secrete bicarbonate, which neutralizes the acid.</li>\n<li>Glucagon-like peptide 1 (GLP-1) and peptide YY: signal fullness (satiety) to the brain.</li>\n</ul>","s":"The small intestine","sn":5,"pn":"5.3"},{"l":"HIST-11","h":"Duodenum and jejunum","p":"<p>All three parts of the small intestine have villi and crypts. Each has one <b>signature feature</b>.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G018-36.jpg\" alt=\"Duodenum (Brunner glands), jejunum (plicae), ileum (Peyer patches)\"><figcaption>Duodenum (Brunner glands), jejunum (plicae), ileum (Peyer patches) <small>· 2026 digestive lab deck, slide 36</small></figcaption><div class=\"lgd\"><span class=\"lgl\">Labels</span><b>dashed line</b> border between villi (above) and crypts (below)<span class=\"sep\">·</span><b>arrow</b> Brunner gland duct emptying into the base of a crypt<span class=\"sep\">·</span><b>P</b> plica circularis (fold with a submucosa core)<span class=\"sep\">·</span><b>SM</b> submucosa<span class=\"sep\">·</span><b>M</b> muscularis externa<span class=\"sep\">·</span><b>large box</b> one Peyer patch lymphoid nodule with the mucosa over it<span class=\"sep\">·</span><b>small box</b> edge of a lymphoid nodule against the submucosa</div></figure>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<tr><th>Segment</th><th>Signature</th><th>Other clues</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Duodenum</b></td><td><b>Brunner glands</b> in the submucosa</td><td>Broad, leaf-like villi. Outer coat: serosa on the first part, adventitia on parts 2–4.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Jejunum</b></td><td>The <b>tallest, most numerous plicae circulares</b>, with a thin, plain submucosa and no glands</td><td>Long, finger-like villi; serosa</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Ileum</b></td><td><b>Peyer patches</b> in the lamina propria and submucosa (5.5)</td><td>Shorter villi; the most goblet cells of the small intestine; serosa</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<h4>Brunner glands</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Branched tubular <b>mucous glands in the submucosa</b> of the duodenum. Their ducts cross the muscularis mucosae and open into the crypts.</li>\n<li>They secrete <b>alkaline mucus</b> (pH about 9). It neutralizes the acid chyme arriving from the stomach and protects the duodenal lining.</li>\n<li>Exclusive to the duodenum. The only other glands in the submucosa of the tube are the esophageal glands.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>On a slide</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Villi plus pale mucous glands filling the submucosa: duodenum.</li>\n<li>Villi on tall folds with thin cores, and nothing in the submucosa: jejunum.</li>\n<li>Villi plus large lymphoid nodules: ileum.</li>\n<li>The pylorus also has mucous glands, but they sit in the mucosa under deep pits, and there are no villi.</li>\n</ul>","s":"The small intestine","sn":5,"pn":"5.4"},{"l":"HIST-11","h":"The colon's lining","p":"<p>The large intestine runs from the cecum through the ascending, transverse, descending (left) and sigmoid colon to the rectum. Its jobs are to <b>absorb water and electrolytes</b> and to form stool. Nutrients have already been absorbed in the small intestine.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Its absorptive cells pump sodium (Na+) out of the lumen actively. Water follows passively by osmosis.</li>\n</ul>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G018-44.jpg\" alt=\"Large intestine mucosa: straight crypts, many goblet cells, no villi\"><figcaption>Large intestine mucosa: straight crypts, many goblet cells, no villi <small>· 2026 digestive lab deck, slide 44</small></figcaption><div class=\"lgd\"><span class=\"lgl\">Labels</span><b>arrows</b> gland openings on the surface<span class=\"sep\">·</span><b>boxes</b> top: surface and gland tops, many goblet cells; bottom: gland bases at the muscularis mucosae<span class=\"sep\">·</span><b>Gl</b> intestinal glands (crypts)<span class=\"sep\">·</span><b>LP</b> lamina propria<span class=\"sep\">·</span><b>bracket (right edge)</b> mucosa (label cut off)<span class=\"sep\">·</span><b>SubM</b> submucosa<span class=\"sep\">·</span><b>MM</b> muscularis mucosae<span class=\"sep\">·</span><b>(d) L</b> folded cell-membrane leaflets in widened spaces between cells (water transport)<span class=\"sep\">·</span><b>(c) G</b> goblet cells</div></figure>\n<h4>Mucosa</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Simple columnar epithelium. The surface is smooth and flat, with <b>no villi</b> and no plicae.</li>\n<li>Straight, closely packed tubular <b>crypts of Lieberkühn</b> reach down to the muscularis mucosae. Cut across, they look like rows of round rings.</li>\n<li>Cells of the crypts:\n<ul>\n<li>Colonocytes (absorptive cells): short microvilli; wide spaces between neighboring cells, a sign of water transport.</li>\n<li><b>Goblet cells</b>: very many, more than anywhere else in the tube. Their mucus lubricates the drying stool and protects the lining.</li>\n<li>A few enteroendocrine cells.</li>\n<li>Stem cells at the base of the crypts.</li>\n</ul></li>\n<li>Lamina propria: rich in MALT (lymphocytes and lymphoid nodules).</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>On a slide</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Goblet cells: pale, round, mucus-filled cups with the nucleus squeezed to the base.</li>\n<li>Stem cells: small, dark cells at the bottom of the crypts, sometimes dividing.</li>\n<li>A flat surface, straight crypts full of goblet cells, and no villi: colon.</li>\n</ul>","s":"The large intestine, appendix and anal canal","sn":6,"pn":"6.1"},{"l":"HIST-11","h":"The colon's wall: teniae coli and diverticula","p":"<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G016-26.jpg\" alt=\"Layers of the large intestine, with a tenia coli in the outer muscle layer\"><figcaption>Layers of the large intestine, with a tenia coli in the outer muscle layer <small>· 2026 lecture: H15B Digestive Tract, slide 26</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Teniae coli</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>In the colon, the <b>outer longitudinal layer of the muscularis externa</b> is gathered into <b>three narrow ribbons</b>, the teniae coli. Between them the longitudinal layer is very thin.</li>\n<li>The inner circular layer stays complete.</li>\n<li>The teniae are shorter than the colon, so they bunch it into pouches (haustra).</li>\n<li>The appendix and the rectum have no teniae; there the longitudinal layer is complete again.</li>\n<li>On a cross-section of colon: three thick bundles of longitudinal muscle, cut across, on the outside of the circular layer.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Outer coat</h4>\n<p>Serosa where the colon hangs free (transverse and sigmoid colon). Adventitia where it is fixed to the back wall (ascending and descending colon).</p>\n<h4>Diverticula</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Small pouches of mucosa and submucosa pushed out through weak spots in the muscularis externa, where blood vessels pass through the wall, between the teniae.</li>\n<li>Most common in the sigmoid colon of older adults (high pressure inside, low-fiber diet).</li>\n<li>Diverticulosis can bleed: sudden, painless, bright red blood from the rectum. Inflamed diverticula (diverticulitis) cause left lower abdominal pain and fever.</li>\n</ul>","s":"The large intestine, appendix and anal canal","sn":6,"pn":"6.2"}]};